  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis indicate broad ridging \nover the Desert Southwest as a shortwave enters the Pacific \nNorthwest. At the surface, high pressure resides over the \nNorthern/Central Plains. A tightened pressure gradient continues to \nbe observed particularly across southeast Kansas with a surface low \nshifting eastward across the Ohio Valley. As a result, breezy north \nwinds continue to be noted across areas primarily east of \nInterstate 135 early this afternoon and will persist into the \nearly evening hours. Low temperatures are once again forecast in\nthe low to mid 60s heading into Sunday morning thanks to the \nslightly cooler and drier airmass in place. As the surface high \nshifts east toward the Great Lakes, winds will gradually turn to\nthe east and then southeast by Sunday evening. Despite this \nturning throughout the day, slightly below average highs ranging\nfrom the mid 80s to low 90s continue to be expected to close \nout the weekend.\n\nIncreasing heights and thicknesses arising from the building of a \nweak upper ridge centered near the Four Corners will promote a \nwarming trend this coming week. Short-term guidance indicates \nhigh temperatures back in the middle to upper 90s for areas \nalong and west of I-135 on Monday, and these are expected to \noverspread a majority of the area by Tuesday. Additionally, some\nlocations may even see highs near 100 degrees especially toward\nthe end of the work week. Per the prior discussion, despite \nhigh temperatures being above average for this time of year, \ndewpoints remaining in the upper 50s and middle 60s should \nlargely prevent heat indices from climbing too far above 100 \ndegrees.\n\nConcerning rainfall, conditions should remain largely dry through \nthe beginning of the week thanks to large-scale subsidence from the \nupper ridge. A slight change to this pattern looks to arrive in the \narea by midweek, as model consensus highlights a stalled out frontal \nboundary setting up across Kansas into Nebraska due to a \nmid/upper wave traversing the Northern Plains. This feature will\nprovide chances for showers and storms across the broader \nregion early on in the week, but for now, better chances for \nrain across the forecast area appear to arrive during the \nafternoon and evening hours Wednesday through Friday. \nUncertainty remains regarding coverage and intensity of any \nactivity that we may see, so stay tuned to upcoming forecasts as\nadditional information becomes available.",
